Integration test against a private Tor network

run.sh builds a small Tor network with chutney (four directory authorities,
three exits with different exit policies and exit addresses, one client),
runs TorDNSEL against the client with active exit testing, and checks the DNS
and HTTP answers, including that an exit whose traffic leaves from an address
other than the advertised one is found by active testing.

  git clone https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/chutney.git
  cabal build
  sudo tests/integration/run.sh chutney $(cabal list-bin tordnsel)

Requirements: tor and tor-gencert (Tor 0.4.8 or newer), python3 (with
"pip install pyasyncore pyasynchat" on Python 3.12 and newer), dig, curl and
iproute2. Root is needed to add 10.200.0.{1,11,12,13,99} to the loopback
interface; run as a normal user with SUDO=sudo to use sudo for just that.
The script uses ports 5000-5007, 7000-7003, 8000-8007, 9000-9007, 15353 and
18080 on the loopback interface. KEEP=1 keeps the logs and Tor data.

A run takes a few minutes, mostly waiting for the network to bootstrap and for
the exit tests (which run every 20 seconds or so).

chutney/ holds the network definition and torrc templates, copied over a
fresh copy of chutney at each run.
